tube_bank.prompt:
Set up a LAMINAR incompressible-flow simulation with the 'incompressibleFluid' solver. Air (kinematic viscosity nu = 1.5e-5 m^2/s) enters a tube bank at 1 m/s along +x and leaves at fixed (zero) pressure; the walls and tubes are no-slip. The mesh geometry is ALREADY staged in the case directory — do NOT author blockMeshDict or snappyHexMeshDict.

IMPORTANT: a patch reported with role 'empty' is a thin-slab front/back face that this 3-D mesh realises as a SYMMETRY patch, so its U and p boundary condition MUST be `type symmetry` (never `type empty`, which would mismatch the mesh patch type).

Author and save the remaining case with the MCP tools, in this order:
  1. call `case_patches` on the case directory to get the boundary patch names and roles;
  2. call `list_configs` to get the EXACT config names — use those names verbatim (do not guess OpenFOAM file names);
  3. call `config_schema` for any config whose shape you are unsure of;
  4. call `save_case` with solver 'incompressibleFluid' and the case directory as target_dir, filling: 0/U and 0/p (one boundary condition per patch, chosen from its role), constant/transportProperties (Newtonian, nu 1.5e-5), constant/turbulenceProperties (laminar), a PIMPLE system/fvSchemes and system/fvSolution, and system/controlDict (pimpleFoam, endTime 0.5, deltaT 0.002, writeControl timeStep, writeInterval 50).

Requirements for a runnable, stable case: for div(phi,U) use a BOUNDED convection scheme such as `Gauss linearUpwind grad(U)` (plain `Gauss linear` diverges on this flow); also include `div((nuEff*dev2(T(grad(U))))) Gauss linear` and `laplacian(nuEff,U) Gauss linear corrected`. Every fvSolution solver entry needs a smoother (GAMG+GaussSeidel for p/p_rgh, smoothSolver+symGaussSeidel for U); PIMPLE also needs pFinal / UFinal / p_rghFinal entries. Each *Final entry is a FULL solver spec — repeat the same solver, smoother and tolerance as its base entry, differing only by relTol 0. Do NOT write a *Final entry with just relTol.

heat_exchanger.prompt:
Set up a CFD simulation of a HEAT EXCHANGER for the 'incompressibleFluid' solver. The working fluid is WATER flowing at about 10 m/s. A heat exchanger transfers heat between streams, so temperature and heat transfer are part of the problem. The mesh geometry is ALREADY staged in the case directory — do NOT author blockMeshDict or snappyHexMeshDict.

YOU decide the physics. Use the MCP tools:
  1. call `case_patches` to get the boundary patches and their roles;
  2. call `model_catalog` to see which physical models this solver offers (turbulence, transport, buoyancy / heat transfer) and CHOOSE the ones appropriate for water in a heat exchanger at 10 m/s — reason about the flow regime (Reynolds number of water at 10 m/s) and whether heat transfer, a temperature field and buoyancy are needed;
  3. call `list_configs` and `config_schema` to learn the exact config names and shapes for the models you selected (including any temperature / turbulence fields they add);
  4. call `save_case` (solver 'incompressibleFluid', target_dir = the case directory) to write a complete case for YOUR chosen physics, using water fluid properties.
  5. after saving, call `validate_case` on the case directory; if it reports any findings, apply the suggested `fix` for each (re-save the affected configs with `save_case`) and call `validate_case` again — repeat until it reports ok=true.

Before saving, briefly state which physical models you selected and WHY — the flow regime and turbulence model, whether you enabled heat transfer / a temperature field / buoyancy, and the water properties you used.
